# How these patterns fit together

This is the **architect-level view**.

You don't normally select just one pattern.

A real enterprise application could look like:

```text
                    Clients
                       │
                       ↓
                 API Gateway
                       │
        ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
        ↓              ↓              ↓
     Ordering       Payment       Inventory
     Context        Context         Context
        │              │              │
     Clean/          Hexagonal      Clean/
     Hexagonal       Architecture   Hexagonal
        │              │              │
        └──────────────┼──────────────┘
                       ↓
                 Event Broker
                       │
                ┌──────┼──────┐
                ↓      ↓      ↓
             Email  Analytics Audit

                  CQRS
                   +
             Event-driven

                  Saga
                   ↓
          Distributed Workflow
```

So an architect might say:

> "We use a domain-oriented microservices style, with bounded contexts derived using DDD. Each service follows Clean/Hexagonal Architecture internally. CQRS is applied selectively for read-heavy domains, asynchronous integration uses event-driven architecture, and Saga handles cross-service workflows. API Gateway provides the external boundary, while Strangler Fig is used to incrementally migrate legacy capabilities."

**That is much stronger than simply saying "I know microservices."**

